00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.520
Now, with that, let's get
started with passwords.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.650
So what's wrong with passwords?

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.620
For starters, weak
passwords are everywhere.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.090
In 2015, a study based on the
Verizon data breach analysis

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.840
showed that these are the two
most popular passwords that

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.290
were found in that breach.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.770
The thing is, three years later,
in 2018, when the same analysis

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:54.750
was done based on more
updated data breaches,

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.368
the situation is still the same.

00:01:56.368 --> 00:01:57.910
I don't know how
many of you noticed,

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:03.450
but the date on the slide,
it changed from 2015 to 2018.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.330
Now, the way
organizations typically

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.720
deal with these weak passwords
is by enforcing complexity

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.318
rules and composition rules.

00:02:11.318 --> 00:02:12.860
Essentially all of
us have seen them.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.460
Passwords should require one
uppercase, one lowercase, one

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.530
number, and so on and so forth.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.320
The problem with these
composition rules,

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.215
firstly, is that it's
a pain for users.

00:02:22.215 --> 00:02:23.340
Users are super frustrated.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.423
If you don't trust me,
just look at how frustrated

00:02:25.423 --> 00:02:28.140
that user looks.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.390
The other part about
these complexity rules

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:31.740
is they don't even work.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.640
Research has shown that when
you have hardwired composition

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.940
rules like this, users
deal with them in extremely

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.010
predictable ways, like they
replace A with the @ symbol,

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.713
they add an exclamation mark at
the end of the word, and so on.

00:02:45.713 --> 00:02:47.130
So there are very
predictable ways

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.650
in which users to deal with
these composition rules.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.400
And so they don't really
add anything significant

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.410
in terms of
security, but it just

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.070
adds frustration for users.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:02.700
So right now, the
security best practices

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.190
is to take away the
burden from the users

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.400
and offload the burden
onto the services

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.280
so that service providers
like Google and others

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.620
can enforce what are
strong passwords based

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.420
on current understanding
of security

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.580
best practices,
comparing passwords

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.610
with password breach databases,
and so on and so forth.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:26.430
Now, even if you have overcome
the weak password problem

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.830
using security best
practices, a strong password

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.690
is no good if it is stolen.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.950
And passwords are
stolen all the time.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.270
So based on our research,
here are the top three ways

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.250
in which passwords are stolen.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.600
No surprise there.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.660
Data breaches of
third-party services,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.760
malware that log your passwords
as they're being typed

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.420
and exfiltrate them,
and the big one--

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.580
phishing.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.890
The reason I said phishing
is the big one compared

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.330
to the other ones is
also going back to--

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.330
is based on some research
we did a few years ago

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.830
when we keep updating
that research.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:05.120
We looked at a user who was
prone to a phishing attack.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.620
And let's say the credential
was compromised in the phishing

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:08.120
attack.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.458
And we compared the likelihood
of such users getting

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:12.000
compromised completely,
that is, they

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.860
are losing access
to their account as

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.589
compared to a randomly-selected
Gmail account.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:21.130
So the hijacking likelihood of
a phished user was 500 times

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.680
a normal user, or
randomly selected user.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.680
This likelihood is
significantly lower

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.690
for other forms of
credential compromise.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.780
And in particular, you can
see if your password was found

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.720
in a breach, it's
only 10 times worse,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.540
whereas if you were phished,
it is 500 times worse.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:40.920
The reason for this difference
is also interesting.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.170
This is just one example
of why or one reason

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.110
why there's a significant
difference between them.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:49.163
It is because, as
a service provider,

00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:50.580
we can provide
certain protections

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.430
against leaked passwords that
don't apply in the phishing

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.020
case.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.320
This is part of that same
program we did a few years ago.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.300
We found compromised credentials
from various breaches.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.280
We found a certain
number of them

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.700
were actually valid passwords
for their Gmail accounts,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.610
and we were immediately able
to lock those accounts down.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.640
Now, this kind of a thing,
it is a batch process.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.080
It's not possible in a phishing
attack where, by the time

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.840
we come to know if it,
it's probably too late.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.820
Phishing is also becoming more
important relative to malware.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.280
It is becoming more and more
possible because of automated

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.770
phishing kits that are available
that even phish multifactor,

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.720
as we will see in a moment.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.690
And malware is becoming
harder to write,

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.850
and phishing is just
brutally effective.

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:41.670
So phishing is just-- there
are just more phishing

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:44.140
sites and more phishing
kits available now as

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:45.570
compared to malware.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:48.000
So it's not all
bad news, though.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.670
The good news here
is, at Google,

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:54.750
even if a malicious actor
has a username and password,

00:05:54.750 --> 00:06:00.360
in 99.9% of the cases, Google
is able to do some risk analysis

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.260
based on geographical location,
device security, history,

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:09.850
and so on, and block 99% of
those malicious accesses.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.690
But the 0.1% are also important.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:17.080
So the answer for
those 0.1% typically

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.880
has been multi-factor
authentication.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.900
Now, multi-factor
authentication methods,

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:24.280
many of them are available--

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:28.660
SMS, TOTP, mobile push,
and so on and so forth.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.670
But many of them have
their own problems.

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:33.190
Usability is one of them.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:38.760
Phishability is the bigger of
the two, because in some sense,

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.340
an OTP is also something
that a user knows.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.320
It's not a long-lived
knowledge, but it's

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:45.940
a short-lived
knowledge that you just

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.940
learned maybe a minute ago.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.250
And it can be phished
in exactly the same way

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:54.160
that the primary
factor can be phished.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:00.100
So the OTPs and most the
traditional MFA methods

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:04.750
are not really the solution
to all of the problems.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:06.460
So the right way
to think about MFA

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:09.610
is that it provides a
spectrum of assurance.

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.530
MFA methods are
not all the same.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:15.160
So here's what we
would recommend.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.960
Any MFA is better than no MFA.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.340
That's number one.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.360
The second part of it is, of
all the available methods,

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.320
if you put them on a scale,
SMS and voice-based codes

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.900
are the least secure
of these methods

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.420
because not only
are they phishable,

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.420
but they also have
other problems

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:35.560
that are now well known,
which include [? SSM ?]

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:39.500
vulnerabilities, carrier social
engineering attacks, and so on.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.590
So it's widely now
known that SMS is

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.240
the weakest of all of the lot.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:46.570
But it's still
better than nothing.

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:49.230
The second bucket
of MFA methods,

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:50.760
we have all the other codes.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:53.250
We have backup codes,
Google authenticated

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.660
or TOTP-based apps, and
then mobile push methods

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.400
such as Google prompt.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.760
These don't have all
of the problems of SMS.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:05.490
But still, all these
three can be phished.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:08.010
And increasingly we're finding
phishing attacks or phishing

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.800
kits that make it
very easy for these

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.000
to be phished in real time.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.150
So the answer to
this phishing problem

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.780
essentially is hardware
FIDO security keys.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:20.670
And these are the
only MFA method

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.460
that have been designed to
be resistant to phishing

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.030
from the ground up.

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:28.230
So to talk more
about FIDO, I'm going

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:30.243
to call Christiaan to
come up to the stage.

00:08:30.243 --> 00:08:31.410
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: Hi, folks.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:32.370
I'm Christiaan.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:34.470
And I'm a product
manager at Google

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:38.100
working on strong authentication
and security keys.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.470
So I want to tell
you a little bit more

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:45.150
about what we do at Google to
prevent phishing, or at least

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:46.590
try and curb it.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:51.000
We've had these security keys
in use on Google accounts

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.978
for the better part
of three, four years.

00:08:53.978 --> 00:08:55.770
And we've even made a
statement last year--

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.950
I believe it was during Next--
where we said that no Google

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:02.550
account has successfully been
phished or at least Googler--

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.320
Google stuff-- has
essentially been

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:08.250
phished since we've deployed the
security key technology, which

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:11.860
is really the primary
purpose of this.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:14.555
The core issue, really,
that users are struggling

00:09:14.555 --> 00:09:15.930
with nowadays when
authenticating

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:19.470
is that they arrive at a website
and enter their credentials,

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.500
but they have no way of knowing
that that website is really

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:25.170
the correct, legitimate website
that they're interacting with.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:28.440
Yes, there is TLS certificates
that you can go and validate.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.110
There are telltale
signs, hopefully,

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:32.310
in the URL address bar.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:33.820
But these things are tricky.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:37.980
And if you go by the security
zone down on the show floor,

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.000
we have two laptops set
up next to each other.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.292
One is at a phishing website,
and the other one is not.

00:09:42.292 --> 00:09:43.830
And it's very,
very hard to tell.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:45.360
It just is.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:46.857
Most of us here
at the conference

00:09:46.857 --> 00:09:48.690
are security professionals,
and even for us,

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:52.860
it is tricky, especially if
that's not the only thing

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.530
that we want to do
on that web page.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:56.358
It's not all about
validating URLs for us.

00:09:56.358 --> 00:09:57.900
We actually want to
get the job done.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:58.910
We want to send an email.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:09:59.660
We want to log in.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:01.360
We want to get access to a file.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:06.053
So we're not spending that much
time checking URLs every day.

00:10:06.053 --> 00:10:08.220
And that really is kind of
the primary problem here.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:09.750
And security keys are
meant to address that.

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:12.030
We specifically developed
the security key technology,

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:16.330
together with industry, to
solve and address that problem.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:18.230
And the way that that works is--

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:18.810
there we go.

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:22.500
The way that that works
is the security key

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:24.780
basically communicates
locally to the hosts.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:26.280
In other words, you plug it in.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:27.930
There is information
being sent from the host

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:28.770
to the security key.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:32.640
It does validation also based on
the origin, like where you are,

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:33.870
where you're browsing to.

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.810
And it will not release
the signatures, as you say,

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.748
until it recognizes that
it's on the correct website

00:10:39.748 --> 00:10:41.040
that it wants to interact with.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.123
And we'll look at that in
a little bit more detail

00:10:43.123 --> 00:10:44.680
here in a couple of slides.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:45.622
So very, very simple--

00:10:45.622 --> 00:10:47.830
if you're on the correct
URL, the security key works.

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:49.020
If not, it doesn't.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.093
Pretty straightforward.

00:10:51.093 --> 00:10:53.260
It's created with open
standards, as I said earlier.

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:56.730
We work in two standards bodies,
in standards organizations.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.680
The one is in the W3C--

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:00.000
World Wide Web Consortium.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.740
And recently, we reached
W3C recommendation status.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:07.980
That means, usually, that
web browsers look at what

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.410
the W3C is coming up with.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:13.590
Typically, also, are involved
in the standardization process.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:15.180
And they usually
pick up standards

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:18.330
that reach recommended status
recommendation in the W3C.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.810
And that is why we've seen folks
like Edge, Mozilla Firefox,

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:24.320
of course Chrome, as well
as Safari in Tech Preview

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:27.780
actually take this seriously
and start doing implementation.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.180
We've recently moved over,
I guess, about three,

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:33.190
four weeks ago, in anticipation
of the launch today, which

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:34.650
I'll show a demo about.

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:38.850
We've moved over to the W3C
standard, as opposed to what

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:44.310
we were doing before, which was
kind of like a pre-fully W3C

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:47.370
rectified standard called
U2F, which is basically

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:48.870
the protocol
between a web server

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.778
and your logged-in device.

00:11:50.778 --> 00:11:52.320
So if you were a
website and you want

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.030
to implement this
cool new stuff here,

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:56.423
you need to understand
the W3C WebAuthn protocol.

00:11:56.423 --> 00:11:58.090
There is another
protocol involved here,

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:00.810
which is called CTAP, the Client
To Authenticator Protocol.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.670
That is still being
standardized in FIDO.

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.430
But a lot less, I guess,
folks in the world

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:07.530
really need to know
about that protocol.

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:11.250
That's the one that your device,
your browser, your platform

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.710
needs to communicate
to the security key.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.460
One way to communicate
is using USB.

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:17.310
There's a standardized
framing and a format

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:18.792
for doing this communication.

00:12:18.792 --> 00:12:20.250
If you are interested
in developing

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:21.725
security keys,
then, of course, you

00:12:21.725 --> 00:12:23.100
need to understand
that protocol.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:24.660
But if you're a web developer
and you want implement this

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:26.490
on your website, you
absolutely don't.

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:28.755
You only need to
understand WebAuthn.

00:12:28.755 --> 00:12:30.630
If you want to make
security keys, of course,

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:31.650
it's an open standard.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:32.788
Anyone can participate.

00:12:32.788 --> 00:12:34.080
Anyone can make a security key.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:37.260
And Google, as folks
know, will pretty much

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:39.415
accept any security
key from any vendor.

00:12:39.415 --> 00:12:41.040
As long as you adhere
to the standards,

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:44.740
that key will work
on Google properties.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:47.840
So we'll talk a little bit more
about that particular protocol

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:48.680
here in a second.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:51.530
But the important
thing here is, this

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:54.830
is mediated by Chrome or by
your browser or your platform

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:55.928
on your machine--

00:12:55.928 --> 00:12:56.720
this communication.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.820
And then it goes over
either USB, NFC--

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:02.060
Near-Field Communication--
or Bluetooth Low Energy.

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:05.405
Those are the three standardized
transports on that level.

00:13:05.405 --> 00:13:07.280
So that's kind of like
if you were interested

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:11.330
how Fido and W3C both
fit into this process,

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:13.190
a little bit of both in there.

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:16.240
Last year at Next, we introduced
the Titan Security Key.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:17.510
It is one more option.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:19.677
There are security keys out
there from many vendors.

00:13:19.677 --> 00:13:22.370
Anyone can make
security keys that

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:24.590
behaves according
to the standard

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:28.060
and works with property
supporting WebAuthn or security

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:29.010
keys.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:32.540
But we wanted to introduce one
more option, one option that

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:35.310
basically has a little bit
of Google goodness in there.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.845
And that's what we did in
the Titan Security Key.

00:13:37.845 --> 00:13:40.220
Essentially, as we said, it's
a phishing-resistant second

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:40.580
factor.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:42.060
We've said that
a bunch of times.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:44.570
But more importantly, it
includes a secure element

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:48.530
with firmware that was written
by Google, which you can then

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:51.020
use, and gives you
this that little bit

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.270
of extra goodness in there.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:54.280
It's an open ecosystem.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:55.947
The key will work
with any other website

00:13:55.947 --> 00:13:57.280
that understands WebAuthn.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:59.600
And there's many of these
different websites out there

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:00.780
today.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:04.880
Here's a more detailed slide on
how the security key actually

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:06.860
works when you're
trying to log in.

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:07.880
So I'll go through this.

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:09.380
And if there's
questions at the end,

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.040
we can delve in a
little bit deeper.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:13.207
But I do think it's important
to at least understand

00:14:13.207 --> 00:14:14.780
this at the superficial level.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:17.540
So this gets one
level below just

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:19.250
understanding that
there's coms happening

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:20.970
between these devices.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:25.365
So how this works is, when I
try to log into www.google.com,

00:14:25.365 --> 00:14:27.740
of course, my phone will send
that request to the server.

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:29.157
And the very next
thing the server

00:14:29.157 --> 00:14:31.670
will do once I've entered
my username and my password,

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:34.670
the server will generate
a cryptographic challenge.

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:36.928
Now, in this example,
we assume that the user

00:14:36.928 --> 00:14:38.720
will have already
registered their security

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:42.050
key with their Google
account at some point in time

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:43.670
before this happens.

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:45.690
The registration is very simple.

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:48.500
The key generates a unique
public-private key key pair.

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:50.300
It gives the public
key to the service--

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:51.675
Google, in this
case-- and Google

00:14:51.675 --> 00:14:53.970
will store that against
this particular account.

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:57.260
So Alice, in this example,
is trying to sign in.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:00.560
And Google knows that there is
a particular public key that

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:04.638
belongs to Alice on file
at the server at Google.

00:15:04.638 --> 00:15:06.680
And we send a cryptographic
challenge, basically,

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:07.555
for freshness, right?

00:15:07.555 --> 00:15:08.972
We say, here are
some random data.

00:15:08.972 --> 00:15:10.930
You're going to need to
do something with that.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:11.980
We just pass that along.

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:13.397
So the random data
is passed along

00:15:13.397 --> 00:15:16.400
to the client-- in
this case, the phone.

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:17.900
The very next thing
that happens is,

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:22.430
Chrome looks at, or the browser
looks at the URL bar or the web

00:15:22.430 --> 00:15:25.850
origin, and sees, ah,
this is for Google.com.

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:26.900
OK, that's cool.

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:28.595
So we're going to remember that.

00:15:28.595 --> 00:15:30.470
And then we're going to
send that information

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:31.887
on over the CTAP protocol.

00:15:31.887 --> 00:15:34.470
Remember, everything happening
on the left side of the picture

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:35.965
is W3C WebAuthn.

00:15:35.965 --> 00:15:37.340
On the right side
of the picture,

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:39.117
that communication is
happening via CTAP.

00:15:39.117 --> 00:15:41.450
So we're going to send this
information from the browser

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:44.717
to the security key
over USB, in this case.

00:15:44.717 --> 00:15:47.300
And we're going to include the
challenge that the server sent.

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:48.755
But we're also
going to include--

00:15:48.755 --> 00:15:49.880
kind of like the caller ID.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:53.477
Like, who is the actual property
asking for the signature?

00:15:53.477 --> 00:15:54.560
We'll look at the URL bar.

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:56.518
We'll strip that out,
and we'll send that along

00:15:56.518 --> 00:15:59.000
over the USB protocol,
to the security key.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.570
Security key will then generate
a cryptographic signature.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.250
So basically, it
takes a challenge.

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:07.190
It signs it using
the private key

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:09.650
that it generated
over a year ago

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:12.190
or whenever we
registered the key.

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:13.670
It generates this signature.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:16.160
And it sends the
signature all the way back

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:21.350
via the USB protocol and via
WebAuthn all the way back

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:22.310
to the server.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:25.550
The server looks at this, sees
that the challenge that it sent

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:26.640
is correct.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.850
It is signed by the private key
that the security key knows.

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:31.850
The server doesn't know
the private key, though.

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:33.392
The server only
knows the public key.

00:16:33.392 --> 00:16:34.933
But using public-private
cryptography

00:16:34.933 --> 00:16:36.440
or public-private
key cryptography.

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:40.430
It can verify that the key
knows the correct secret

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:43.580
without ever exposing that
secret to the server itself.

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:45.463
How great is that
for data breaches?

00:16:45.463 --> 00:16:47.380
If something happens
with the server nowadays,

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:49.050
your passwords are exposed.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:49.950
We can run through.

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:51.590
It doesn't matter if the
password is hashed or whatever.

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:53.673
Like rainbow tables and
other stuff are out there,

00:16:53.673 --> 00:16:55.010
passwords get cracked.

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:57.343
Because of the fact that we
use symmetric authentication

00:16:57.343 --> 00:16:59.593
on the web today, your
password's going to be cracked.

00:16:59.593 --> 00:17:01.010
In this example,
even if someone--

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:03.051
if there is a data breach
over at the server side

00:17:03.051 --> 00:17:05.690
and things get compromised, yes,
they'll have your user name.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:06.815
They'll have your password.

00:17:06.815 --> 00:17:08.660
But they will never
have your private key,

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:10.819
because that private key is
never stored on the server.

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:12.444
The public key is
stored on the server.

00:17:12.444 --> 00:17:14.359
That allows the server
to know that you

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:15.770
have the right security key.

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:18.560
But it never allows the
server to impersonate

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:22.398
that said security key, which
is really cool, really nifty.

00:17:22.398 --> 00:17:24.440
The other thing, of course,
here is that we sign.

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:25.815
We don't only sign
the challenge.

00:17:25.815 --> 00:17:27.319
We also sign the web origin.

00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:29.510
So the server is
absolutely certain

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:32.030
that you are looking
at the correct website

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:34.970
at the point in time when the
security key was presented.

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:37.040
So both of these two
things are signed together,

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:39.650
both the challenge
and the web origin.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:42.035
And that all get sent
down to the server.

00:17:42.035 --> 00:17:43.910
The user is looking at
the incorrect website,

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:45.848
the incorrect thing
will be signed,

00:17:45.848 --> 00:17:47.390
and the server will
simply reject it.

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:49.340
So pretty straightforward.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:51.600
And we'll look at
something else here

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:52.860
in more detail in a second.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:54.530
So what we're really here is
talk about-- and at this point,

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:55.760
it's kind of old news.

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.070
We've said it from this
morning or this keynote,

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:01.040
and then we revealed it in the
security spotlight session.

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.660
We wanted to make things
a little easier for users.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:07.010
It's great to have multiple
options in security keys--

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:10.880
NFC, USB, maybe a Bluetooth
key that I can use.

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:12.353
But it's still an
additional device

00:18:12.353 --> 00:18:13.520
that I have to carry around.

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:15.140
How cool would it
be if the thing

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:16.370
that's already in my pocket--

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:18.890
my mobile phone-- could
just be my security key

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:20.130
for my Google account?

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:21.250
Now, we're going to say,
well, that's not new.

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:23.375
I've been using my phone
to authenticate for years.

00:18:23.375 --> 00:18:24.380
Well, that's true.

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:27.290
It started out with using SMS
as a one-time password sent

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:28.100
to a phone.

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:30.890
Then we started looking at
authenticator applications

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:33.523
running on the phone,
generating one-time passwords.

00:18:33.523 --> 00:18:35.690
Even push authentication
nowadays, you get a message

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:36.760
and you say yes.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.802
The technology that you're
going to look at right

00:18:38.802 --> 00:18:40.910
now is very similar in
the user experience.

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:43.280
You also look at the phone
and say yes to a message.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.100
The critical difference
is, that message is not

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:47.313
going via the cloud.

00:18:47.313 --> 00:18:48.980
There is some slides
in here later where

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:51.315
we break that whole
log-in process down

00:18:51.315 --> 00:18:52.940
and where we see the
difference between

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:54.830
traditional push-based
authentication

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:56.530
and this technology here.

00:18:56.530 --> 00:18:59.720
Because there is
a local proximity

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:03.170
channel between the laptop
and my mobile phone,

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:06.620
we can apply the
same protocol which

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:09.223
makes sure the user is
looking at the right website

00:19:09.223 --> 00:19:11.390
at the point in time when
they're trying to sign in.

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:13.370
That really is the
breakthrough here--

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:16.045
very, very, very simple concept.

00:19:16.045 --> 00:19:17.420
But obviously,
the implementation

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:19.160
is hard because you have
to deal with Bluetooth

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:21.118
and different browsers
and different platforms.

00:19:21.118 --> 00:19:23.030
But in the end, all
these things make up

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:26.060
a cohesive whole, which
gives you a user experience

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:27.020
that users are used to.

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:28.490
They've been doing it for years.

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:31.550
But this time, really giving
them that elevated threat

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:33.530
protection, the
phishing protection

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:36.425
that security keys and the
protocol have pulled in.

00:19:36.425 --> 00:19:37.700
So pretty straightforward.

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:39.050
And then there's one last
thing here which we'll

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:39.890
talk about a little bit later.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:41.930
If you happen to have
a Pixel 3, last year,

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:43.700
when we announced
the Pixel 3, we also

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:46.380
mentioned that the Pixel 3
has a Titan M chip in there.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:49.370
So there's a cryptographic
secure element built by Google

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:50.780
that does a bunch of things.

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:53.000
In that blog post at the
time, we alluded to the fact

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.350
that, hey, maybe
later this thing

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:55.730
can also be used
as a security key.

00:19:55.730 --> 00:19:56.970
And I guess that time is now.

00:19:56.970 --> 00:20:01.490
So on a Pixel 3, we're actually
using and storing your FIDO key

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:03.590
material-- the
cryptographic material

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:06.170
that we use to generate
these signatures-- directly

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:07.905
in that secure element.

00:20:07.905 --> 00:20:09.530
And the secure element
actually happens

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:11.180
to be hard-wired
through the volume

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:12.890
down button on my
Pixel phone, which

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:15.280
is why I have to press that
button during authentication

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.780
on this particular device, which
means if there is something

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:19.910
else running on the phone, it'll
never be able to physically

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:21.920
press the butto, which is
kind of the same premise

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:22.712
with security keys.

00:20:22.712 --> 00:20:25.135
That's why we require a button
press at the point in time

00:20:25.135 --> 00:20:26.510
when we're going
to do signature.

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:28.885
Even if there's something
else, maybe malicious, running,

00:20:28.885 --> 00:20:31.140
it hopefully will not be
able to physically exercise

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:32.720
my security key's button.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:34.590
But we'll talk about that.

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:37.560
So let's now jump into a demo.

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.220
So what I'm going
to do is, I'm going

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:43.610
to ask folks to switch over
to the laptop right here.

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:46.500
And I'll bring up the
phone here in a second.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:49.705
But let's start-- this is a
user who's signed into Gmail.

00:20:49.705 --> 00:20:52.040
Let's refresh and make
sure everything looks good.

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:53.450
Cool.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:55.417
I already use security keys.

00:20:55.417 --> 00:20:57.500
And this is the state that
we want users to be in.

00:20:57.500 --> 00:21:00.590
We really want users setting up
their phone's built-in security

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:02.720
key to already have
something else that they

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:04.310
can use if this process fails.

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:05.750
That's why we're saying beta--

00:21:05.750 --> 00:21:07.542
not because of the
technology, necessarily,

00:21:07.542 --> 00:21:10.020
having an inherent problem,
but you need Bluetooth for this

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:10.520
to work.

00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:11.960
And you're not always going
to be on a machine that

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:12.750
has Bluetooth.

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:13.720
You have a desktop.

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:15.890
If you sign in on
unsupported platform,

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:19.220
let's say like Windows versions
older than Windows 10--

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:22.250
younger? older than
Windows 10?, so lower--

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:23.757
you might have a
private challenge.

00:21:23.757 --> 00:21:25.340
So the protocol right
now is supported

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:29.210
on pretty much all Android
7-plus phones and Windows 10,

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:32.360
Chrome OS, and Mac OS.

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:33.990
But again, Bluetooth
is a requirement.

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:35.090
So we want you to have a backup.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:36.680
So this user is in
that perfect state.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:40.610
If I click on my account
details over here, and I

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:43.250
review my security settings.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:46.570
You will see, under
two-step verification,

00:21:46.570 --> 00:21:51.360
that this user has a couple of
security keys already setup.

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:54.500
So this user happens to
have a Titan USB key--

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:56.837
one right here-- and a
Titan Bluetooth key--

00:21:56.837 --> 00:21:57.670
this one right here.

00:21:57.670 --> 00:22:00.140
So these things are already
set up on the account.

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:01.880
Today, we see this
promo happening.

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:03.440
And the promo says,
hey, by the way,

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:06.350
you have a compatible Android
phone already on your account.

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:08.497
The way we know this
is, this user already

00:22:08.497 --> 00:22:10.580
signed into their Android
device using this Google

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:12.380
account at some previous time.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:14.580
So we were able to
correlate this and say, hey,

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.270
we think you have a compatible
phone for this technology.

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:18.860
This promo might
take a little while

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:20.027
to show up for your account.

00:22:20.027 --> 00:22:22.820
However, if you just click
the Add Security Key button,

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:25.105
you should immediately be
able to use this technology.

00:22:25.105 --> 00:22:26.730
That was already
released this morning.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:27.990
So if you click
Add Security Key,

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:29.490
which I will do in
this case, you'll

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:30.807
see all your various options.

00:22:30.807 --> 00:22:32.390
If you don't have
any eligible phones,

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:35.520
it'll go straight into the, hey,
add a USB or Bluetooth security

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:36.080
key.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:38.122
In this case, I happen to
have a compatible Pixel

00:22:38.122 --> 00:22:38.810
3 on my account.

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:40.770
If you have more than one
phone, they'll all show up here

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:42.480
and you can pick
what you want to use.

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:44.188
I've got my Pixel 3,
at this point, which

00:22:44.188 --> 00:22:45.570
is what I'm going to activate.

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:46.440
Going to click it.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.030
It will tell me a
bunch of like things

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:49.730
like I need Bluetooth
at the location,

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:52.530
other things on the phone
from this technology to work.

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:53.400
Don't need pairing.

00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:54.870
That's the critical part here.

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:58.960
With these other Bluetooth keys
that we spoke about earlier,

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:00.088
we needed pairing.

00:23:00.088 --> 00:23:02.130
You would need to pair
this device with our phone

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:04.590
or with our laptop
for it to work.

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:08.010
We listened to our users,
and they don't like pairing.

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:08.580
It's hard.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:11.070
If you've ever tried to pair
a phone and a laptop together,

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:12.117
it's not easy.

00:23:12.117 --> 00:23:13.450
We wanted to get away from that.

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:16.660
So we said, what can we do
to make the protocol easier?

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:18.630
So we basically came up
with a little extension

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:22.050
to the WebAuthn protocol
that's standardized right now.

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:24.270
And this extension
allows for pairing

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:26.460
less-secure communication
between your laptop

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:28.110
and your phone.

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:32.760
The protocol extension is being
standardized in the standards

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:35.490
organization FIDO as we speak.

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.080
It has not completely
been ratified yet.

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:39.490
It takes a while.

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:42.372
We've decided, as Chrome, to
do the first implementation.

00:23:42.372 --> 00:23:44.830
Any other browser, of course,
is free to do the same thing.

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:45.930
But we wanted to
get it out there

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:48.263
so we can start testing with
this particular protocol.

00:23:48.263 --> 00:23:50.430
So we expect other browsers
to pick it up right now,

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:51.620
or at some point, too.

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:53.370
But right now, Chrome
is the browser where

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:54.578
this protocol is implemented.

00:23:54.578 --> 00:23:55.893
So anyway, we'll Add.

00:23:55.893 --> 00:23:57.810
In the end, it'll say,
hey, everything's cool.

00:23:57.810 --> 00:23:58.780
So I'll go back.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:00.780
And now I see three
security keys on my account.

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:02.220
I've got my Google Pixel 3.

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:03.510
I've got my USB key.

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:05.790
And I have my Bluetooth key.

00:24:05.790 --> 00:24:08.460
So now, let's see how
this works during sign-in.

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:10.500
So I will close this window.

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:13.380
I will open a new
incognito window.

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:17.910
And at this point, I'm
going to try and display

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:21.190
my Pixel 3 on this screen.

00:24:21.190 --> 00:24:23.930
So let's move that
out of the way.

00:24:23.930 --> 00:24:26.557
So this is my phone
that I have here.

00:24:26.557 --> 00:24:28.140
And I'm going to try
and access Gmail.

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:30.480
So Gmail.com.

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:36.400
I am going to enter my
demo account credentials.

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:37.900
And at this point
in time, I should

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:41.950
see the new UI
appearing, which tells me

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.507
I can use either USB or
Bluetooth, of course.

00:24:44.507 --> 00:24:46.090
But I'm going to
pick to use my phone.

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:47.980
That's the default if you
have a phone on your account.

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:49.333
And we see a message pop up.

00:24:49.333 --> 00:24:50.750
This is a Pixel 3
experience which

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:51.850
says I have to hold
down the Volume

00:24:51.850 --> 00:24:53.240
Down button, which
I'm going to do.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.510
Going to click it and hold it
down until the blue checkmark

00:24:55.510 --> 00:24:55.900
appears.

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:58.480
Going to be a local Bluetooth
connectivity between these two

00:24:58.480 --> 00:24:59.360
devices.

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:01.570
And once that completes,
I will be allowed

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:02.875
to sign in to my account.

00:25:02.875 --> 00:25:04.750
And at this point, it
did all of those checks

00:25:04.750 --> 00:25:05.680
that we spoke about earlier.

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:06.888
There were challenges passed.

00:25:06.888 --> 00:25:07.810
The phone signed it.

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:10.268
The key material happened to
be stored in the Titan M chip.

00:25:10.268 --> 00:25:11.300
Crypto happened there.

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:13.330
And we also made
sure on the way back

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:16.750
that I was actually looking
at the legitimate website

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:19.540
here, the real
mail.google.com and not

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:21.705
some other, malicious
website, perhaps.

00:25:21.705 --> 00:25:23.720
So anyways, so that's the demo.

00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:25.990
So we'll go back to the
slide deck, at this point,

00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:28.210
and we'll see what
happened there.

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:29.895
So how this fit
in-- same picture.

00:25:29.895 --> 00:25:32.270
I'm just going to go through
it really quickly because we

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:33.250
really discussed this already.

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:35.050
And the point of
this picture is just

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:37.240
to show it's exactly
the same technology

00:25:37.240 --> 00:25:39.520
and the same protocol
that we've been using,

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:42.150
like with USB and physical
Bluetooth security

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:43.210
keys in place here.

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:44.050
Same thing.

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:45.060
We're sending data over.

00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:46.810
It can be sent via
other USB, NFC, or BLE.

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:49.660
And at this point,
it will send via BLE.

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:52.310
And phone signed it,
sent it all the way back.

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:53.800
And the server
validated that this

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:58.810
was the correct key-- well,
the correct secret that signed

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:00.350
this cryptographic challenge.

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:03.507
So pretty straightforward.

00:26:03.507 --> 00:26:05.090
I don't have to go
into this too much.

00:26:05.090 --> 00:26:07.000
Of course, it offers a stronger
defense against phishing.

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.583
That's the point
we're trying to make.

00:26:08.583 --> 00:26:11.825
And it very verifies user's
identity and the URL or the URL

00:26:11.825 --> 00:26:12.700
that you're visiting.

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:13.870
This is kind of
the takeaway slide.

00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:15.495
If there's two things
that you remember

00:26:15.495 --> 00:26:18.400
from this presentation, this
is what security keys do.

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:19.570
It's about the protocol.

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:21.370
It's not about the
physical form factor.

00:26:21.370 --> 00:26:24.130
Ever since we started on
the path of security keys,

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:26.980
the intent was always to
bring the thing in your pocket

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:29.150
to be that technology
that you can

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:31.150
use without having to
make the user carry around

00:26:31.150 --> 00:26:33.030
something else.

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:35.830
Of course, security
keys has its place.

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:38.988
You still use that to
bootstrap an account,

00:26:38.988 --> 00:26:40.030
have that as your backup.

00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:41.680
But you now have one
additional option.

00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:44.160
It's just technologically much
easier to make these devices

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:47.480
than implement everything
directly on the device itself,

00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:49.060
inside the phone.

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:54.640
So as one of the
last sections here,

00:26:54.640 --> 00:26:58.300
I want to just show
the difference between

00:26:58.300 --> 00:27:03.460
the nonsecurity key, push-based
authentication protocol

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:05.920
and the push-based
authentic-- or, sorry,

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:07.960
and the BLE-based
authentication protocol,

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:10.890
right So for the push-based
authentication protocol,

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:13.580
the one that goes via
the cloud normally that--

00:27:13.580 --> 00:27:15.610
we offer it as Google.

00:27:15.610 --> 00:27:18.173
The name that we call
it is Google Prompt.

00:27:18.173 --> 00:27:19.590
So you can activate
Google Prompt.

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:23.330
You could set that up as
your 2SV mechanism for ages.

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:25.490
We actually changed it
over a couple of months,

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:27.700
maybe a year ago so
that you can set that up

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:29.160
as your default option.

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:31.452
So if you activate two-step
verification or multifactor

00:27:31.452 --> 00:27:33.880
authentication on a
Google account today,

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:36.410
Google Prompt is the option
that you're going to get.

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:36.970
It's great.

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:38.262
It's very like straightforward.

00:27:38.262 --> 00:27:40.210
It has the same user
experience roughly

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:41.440
as what we've seen here.

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:44.060
But it sends the
messages via the cloud.

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:47.650
So if you look at what's going
on on this diagram right here,

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:49.510
this is how users get tricked.

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:51.617
User goes, and the
user goes to a website.

00:27:51.617 --> 00:27:53.200
At this point, I
call it phishing.com,

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:56.650
but it's a little bit more
subtle than that in real life.

00:27:56.650 --> 00:27:59.127
I end up going to a website
that looks a lot like Google.

00:27:59.127 --> 00:28:00.960
But it's not really the
real Google website.

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:02.335
What's, in essence,
happening is,

00:28:02.335 --> 00:28:05.980
I'm visiting a website that's
proxying all of my information

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:08.473
and all of the data between
the real Google and me.

00:28:08.473 --> 00:28:09.640
So that's what we're seeing.

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:11.688
In step 3, that's Google.

00:28:11.688 --> 00:28:12.980
That's the real Google servers.

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:14.230
However, I'm not
interacting with it.

00:28:14.230 --> 00:28:16.760
I'm interacting with something
that is interacting with it.

00:28:16.760 --> 00:28:20.980
So they're proxying
everything back to my machine

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:22.730
and to my browser.

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:25.035
So what's happening is,
user is interacting.

00:28:25.035 --> 00:28:26.410
They're typing in
their username.

00:28:26.410 --> 00:28:27.580
Bad guy's getting it.

00:28:27.580 --> 00:28:28.955
They're typing in
their password.

00:28:28.955 --> 00:28:30.132
Bad guy's getting it.

00:28:30.132 --> 00:28:31.840
They're sending it on
to the real Google.

00:28:31.840 --> 00:28:35.140
Real Google happily
sends the push message

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:38.750
to the real user, who is trying
to sign in at this point.

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:41.680
But of course, when I
say yes to that message,

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:42.715
I'm not signing me in.

00:28:42.715 --> 00:28:43.840
I'm signing the bad guy in.

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:46.150
And there's no way for Google
to know whether they're

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:48.700
interacting with
the legitimate user

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:51.090
or with someone proxying
everything on the user's

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:51.590
behalf.

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:53.798
And that's really the problem,
and one of the reasons

00:28:53.798 --> 00:28:56.425
why these push messages
contain other metadata.

00:28:56.425 --> 00:28:59.050
It says things like the location
you're trying to sign in from.

00:28:59.050 --> 00:29:00.340
So you might be like,
why's it telling me

00:29:00.340 --> 00:29:01.090
where I'm signing in from?

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:02.465
I know where I'm
signing in from.

00:29:02.465 --> 00:29:04.815
Well, it's because if someone
is proxying that message,

00:29:04.815 --> 00:29:06.940
hopefully, they're not in
the same location as you.

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:08.710
And you'll check that
message and see, oh,

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:10.390
this guy's trying to sign
it from a different country

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:11.410
or a different state.

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:13.390
I'm clearly going to
say no to this message.

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:16.150
Using IPG location,
mostly, so nowadays, it's

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:20.320
very easy for folks to
set up VPN tunnels that

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:22.800
pop out in roughly the
same neighborhood or areas

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:24.340
that you're in.

00:29:24.340 --> 00:29:25.810
So that's another challenge.

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:28.300
There is a way to circumvent
some of these technologies.

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:31.330
Other push authentication
technologies

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:33.820
show you the IP address
of who you're seeing.

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:36.438
Who knows what their IP is
when they're trying to sign in?

00:29:36.438 --> 00:29:37.980
You're not going to
go and check out,

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:39.463
what is my
public-facing IP address

00:29:39.463 --> 00:29:41.130
every time you do one
of these messages.

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:44.300
So even that kind of stuff
doesn't really, really work.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:46.350
Sure, we're putting
more of the burden

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:49.260
back to the user where they
can potentially figure out

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:51.718
whether they're interacting
with a legitimate site or not.

00:29:51.718 --> 00:29:53.010
But it's not that easy to tell.

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:54.260
We wanted to solve that problem.

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:55.080
We wanted to say,
well, why don't we

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.760
make it the technology's problem
and not the user's problem?

00:29:57.760 --> 00:29:59.910
And that's really
where we ended up here.

00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:01.500
Much simpler picture, right?

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:03.900
The user is now looking
at the phishing website.

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:06.420
Bad guy is still up
there to the left.

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:08.730
But this time around,
because the communication

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:11.970
to the mobile phone is directly
from the user's machine

00:30:11.970 --> 00:30:14.430
to the phone and not, as
in the previous picture,

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:17.020
from the Google
Cloud to the phone,

00:30:17.020 --> 00:30:19.740
we're able to tell
the phone the URL

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.822
that the user is looking at.

00:30:21.822 --> 00:30:24.030
And the phone will either
refuse to sign the message,

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:27.390
or we'll sign it but we'll send
back the data to the cloud.

00:30:27.390 --> 00:30:29.010
In this case, what
we're seeing is,

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:32.400
the browser is sending back
phishing.com in that signature,

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:33.988
and Google is going,
no, no, no, no.

00:30:33.988 --> 00:30:36.030
That's not the website
the user should be looking

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:37.550
at when they're logging in.

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:39.923
So really, really
straightforward

00:30:39.923 --> 00:30:41.340
if you think about
the technology.

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:46.090
Again, really, critically
important, but very,

00:30:46.090 --> 00:30:47.550
very simple.

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:50.448
And that's how-- or
at least a difference

00:30:50.448 --> 00:30:52.740
between using traditional
push-based mechanisms, which,

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:55.140
again, there's nothing
wrong with it, inherently.

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:57.790
It's certainly better than
not having any 2SV2 at all,

00:30:57.790 --> 00:31:00.490
and certainly better than
some other 2SV options.

00:31:00.490 --> 00:31:02.430
But if you want
this enhanced level

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:04.923
of protection--
in the early days,

00:31:04.923 --> 00:31:06.090
you had to get one of these.

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:07.350
Now you have one
more option in just

00:31:07.350 --> 00:31:08.520
turning your phone into it.

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:10.570
So the prompt on the phone
looks very much the same.

00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:12.170
If you don't happen to
have a Pixel 3 device,

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:13.712
the prompt actually
looks pretty much

00:31:13.712 --> 00:31:16.875
exactly the same as the
Google Prompt technology.

00:31:16.875 --> 00:31:18.750
If you look at that
screen there on the right

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:21.220
and you look at the one here,
it is very much similar.

00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:23.010
We don't show you any of
the other stuff anymore.

00:31:23.010 --> 00:31:24.810
We don't show you the
location or the IP address

00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:26.220
or anything like that,
because at this point,

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:27.012
it's not important.

00:31:27.012 --> 00:31:28.380
We know these things are local.

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.150
That's the important part.

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:33.613
So to end off and wrap
up here, it's convenient.

00:31:33.613 --> 00:31:36.030
Users are prompted to verify
their sign-in on their phone.

00:31:36.030 --> 00:31:37.685
It's pretty straightforward.

00:31:37.685 --> 00:31:39.310
There is a one-time,
simple enrollment.

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:39.990
We've seen that.

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:40.630
It's pretty straightforward.

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:42.900
You don't need to install
any apps on the phone.

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:45.390
You don't even need to upgrade
the software of your Android

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:46.200
phone.

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:48.090
It happens automatically
in the background.

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:50.010
Your phone should have
it, at this point.

00:31:50.010 --> 00:31:52.142
All Android 7-plus
devices should have this.

00:31:52.142 --> 00:31:54.350
And the way that we technically
get this to the phone

00:31:54.350 --> 00:31:56.892
is an integration with something
called Google Play Services,

00:31:56.892 --> 00:31:59.100
which is like a
back-end component that

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:01.440
lives in Android
phones that interact

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:02.720
with Google properties.

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:05.100
So if you have access to
Gmail and other services

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:07.530
on the device, typically, this
module is already present.

00:32:07.530 --> 00:32:09.550
And it's constantly
updating in the background.

00:32:09.550 --> 00:32:11.133
And that's what we're
using to deliver

00:32:11.133 --> 00:32:15.100
this functionality to devices,
going back a couple of years.

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:17.040
Right now, it's available
to more than 50%.

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:20.610
If you look at the distribution
of Android devices,

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:23.880
more than 50% of
users running 7-plus.

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:26.900
So available to more than
50% of the Android market.

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:27.540
And it's free.

00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:28.590
You don't have to
pay anything for it.

00:32:28.590 --> 00:32:29.548
You can just enable it.

00:32:29.548 --> 00:32:32.100
The only thing you need
is a compatible device.

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:35.710
And as I said earlier,
there's one more thing here,

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:38.850
which is, if you happen to
have a Pixel 3 device which

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:40.980
has the Titan M
chip built into it,

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:43.560
we will actually be using
very much the same way that we

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:46.290
differentiate regular security
keys versus Titan security

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:46.860
keys.

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:49.650
It's kind of the same
way that we differentiate

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:51.870
the built-in security
key on Android

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:55.560
versus the built-in security
key on Android Pixel 3.

00:32:55.560 --> 00:32:56.590
Kind of the same thing.

00:32:56.590 --> 00:32:57.965
It's just a little
bit more trust

00:32:57.965 --> 00:32:59.830
in where those keys
are actually stored

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:01.410
and in the way that
you exercise it.

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:04.340
Again, I don't want to
go into too much detail

00:33:04.340 --> 00:33:07.470
on the differences here and
say that one is necessarily

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:08.530
better than the other.

00:33:08.530 --> 00:33:10.260
Remember, the attack we're
trying to prevent against here

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:11.060
is remote phishing.

00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:13.602
We're trying to prevent someone
that's in a different country

00:33:13.602 --> 00:33:16.440
or in a different
location than you

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:17.940
trying to sign
in, because that's

00:33:17.940 --> 00:33:19.120
the threat that's scalable.

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:20.787
That's the thing that
we see on the web.

00:33:20.787 --> 00:33:24.210
That's also the stuff that you
saw Sriram speak about earlier.

00:33:24.210 --> 00:33:26.040
If we look at the
amount of phishing

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:28.793
websites that's springing up
every month, it's staggering.

00:33:28.793 --> 00:33:30.960
That's the type of thing
we want to protect against.

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:32.670
And for that, it doesn't really
matter where on your phone

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:33.660
the key is stored.

00:33:33.660 --> 00:33:36.810
It can be stored on file
system or in a [? TEE, ?]

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:38.090
or even in the Titan M chip.

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:39.570
It doesn't really
matter that much.

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:42.420
But we do have this
additional level

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:45.660
of protection, the same as with
the physical Titan security

00:33:45.660 --> 00:33:49.620
key for folks who
care about that.

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:50.670
Last slide, here.

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:53.600
It's available now
in beta, as we said.

00:33:53.600 --> 00:33:55.350
It's available on
Android 7 phones,

00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:57.450
works with
Bluetooth-enabled Chrome

00:33:57.450 --> 00:34:01.950
browser on Chrome OS,
Mac OS X, and Windows 10.

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:04.950
And you can use it both on
your personal Gmail account

00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:09.000
as well as on any of
our enterprise accounts.

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:11.340
And if an administrator
has set up an organization

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:16.830
to require a security key, this
is now a valid way to, I guess,

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:19.469
address that particular
constraint, where

00:34:19.469 --> 00:34:21.750
you can set up your
phone, and it'll happily

00:34:21.750 --> 00:34:25.110
satisfy the constraint of using
a security key on that account.

00:34:25.110 --> 00:34:28.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

